1. Creating and Renaming Files/Directories

mkdir test_dir
cd test_dir
touch example.txt
mv example.txt renamed_example.txt

mkdir test_dir creates a new directory named test_dir.


cd test_dir moves inside the created directory.


touch example.txt creates an empty file.


mv example.txt renamed_example.txt renames the file.


2. Viewing File Contents

cat /etc/passwd
head -n 5 /etc/passwd
tail -n 5 /etc/passwd
cat displays the full content of the file.


head -n 5 displays the first 5 lines.


tail -n 5 displays the last 5 lines.


3. Searching for Patterns

grep "root" /etc/passwd

grep is used to search for specific text.


This command displays all lines containing the word root from /etc/passwd.

4. Zipping and Unzipping Files

zip -r test_dir.zip test_dir

mkdir unzipped_dir
unzip test_dir.zip -d unzipped_dir

zip -r compresses the directory recursively.


unzip -d extracts the zip file into the specified directory.

5. Downloading Files

wget https://example.com/sample.txt

wget downloads files from the internet using a URL.

6. Changing File Permissions

touch secure.txt
chmod 444 secure.txt

touch secure.txt creates a new file.


chmod 444 sets read-only permission for owner, group, and others.

7. Working with Environment Variables

export MY_VAR="Hello, Linux!"
echo $MY_VAR

export creates an environment variable.


echo $MY_VAR displays the variable value.
